WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.360 --> 00:00:03.630
- [Narrator] If you've been
waiting on a package for weeks,

00:00:03.630 --> 00:00:05.350
your order might be here,

00:00:05.350 --> 00:00:07.463
stuck at sea in a shipping container.

00:00:08.340 --> 00:00:11.340
The COVID pandemic has
snarled global supply chains

00:00:11.340 --> 00:00:13.520
causing massive backlogs and shortages

00:00:13.520 --> 00:00:16.480
that have some companies
stockpiling goods in trailers

00:00:16.480 --> 00:00:19.080
and chartering private container ships.

00:00:19.080 --> 00:00:21.440
But these are temporary fixes.

00:00:21.440 --> 00:00:23.150
For longer-term solutions,

00:00:23.150 --> 00:00:25.540
some companies are looking
to make sweeping changes

00:00:25.540 --> 00:00:26.860
to their supply chains,

00:00:26.860 --> 00:00:29.860
including shifting
manufacturing closer to home,

00:00:29.860 --> 00:00:31.330
but pulling off these strategies

00:00:31.330 --> 00:00:35.310
can require retooling a complex
web of global production,

00:00:35.310 --> 00:00:38.310
potentially adding new risks and costs.

00:00:38.310 --> 00:00:39.370
First, here's how

00:00:39.370 --> 00:00:42.750
a globally distributed
supply chain typically works.

00:00:42.750 --> 00:00:45.620
In this case, for
Timberland's Pit Boss boots,

00:00:45.620 --> 00:00:49.230
a shoe the company sells to
consumers in the US and beyond.

00:00:49.230 --> 00:00:52.770
Sourcemap, a website that
publishes data about supply chains

00:00:52.770 --> 00:00:54.030
says the materials,

00:00:54.030 --> 00:00:56.300
including the leather and metal details

00:00:56.300 --> 00:00:58.620
are sourced from locations
around the world,

00:00:58.620 --> 00:01:02.130
including the US, the UK, China, Japan,

00:01:02.130 --> 00:01:05.100
South Korea, Taiwan, and Vietnam.

00:01:05.100 --> 00:01:06.310
All of these materials

00:01:06.310 --> 00:01:08.630
are shipped to a factory in Bangladesh

00:01:08.630 --> 00:01:10.220
where the boots are assembled.

00:01:10.220 --> 00:01:11.370
The boots are transported

00:01:11.370 --> 00:01:13.530
to a distribution center in Virginia,

00:01:13.530 --> 00:01:15.690
where they're then shipped
off to retail locations

00:01:15.690 --> 00:01:17.950
and customers around the world.

00:01:17.950 --> 00:01:22.300
- Modern supply chains are
complex and they're far flung.

00:01:22.300 --> 00:01:23.133
- [Narrator] Jen Smith

00:01:23.133 --> 00:01:25.620
has been covering the forces
behind the supply chain issues

00:01:25.620 --> 00:01:27.120
for the "Wall Street Journal."

00:01:27.120 --> 00:01:28.940
She says that over the past few decades,

00:01:28.940 --> 00:01:32.160
supply chains have become
increasingly global.

00:01:32.160 --> 00:01:33.470
- With every step along the way,

00:01:33.470 --> 00:01:35.620
there is the potential for disruption,

00:01:35.620 --> 00:01:38.460
whether it's in manufacturing
or in transport,

00:01:38.460 --> 00:01:40.700
or if you're having
trouble distributing it

00:01:40.700 --> 00:01:42.380
once it gets here.

00:01:42.380 --> 00:01:43.590
- [Narrator] Lately, disruptions

00:01:43.590 --> 00:01:45.460
have caused extensive delays,

00:01:45.460 --> 00:01:48.220
which has driven up
the cost of many goods.

00:01:48.220 --> 00:01:49.053
So some companies

00:01:49.053 --> 00:01:50.870
are looking at different
location strategies

00:01:50.870 --> 00:01:52.870
to protect their supply chain

00:01:52.870 --> 00:01:53.970
and their bottom line.

00:01:55.500 --> 00:01:57.670
One idea being discussed
in corporate boardrooms

00:01:57.670 --> 00:01:59.650
is called regionalization.

00:01:59.650 --> 00:02:01.280
This involves setting up factories

00:02:01.280 --> 00:02:02.830
in multiple parts of the world

00:02:02.830 --> 00:02:04.900
so that operations in each region

00:02:04.900 --> 00:02:08.220
are able to supply products
to the closest markets.

00:02:08.220 --> 00:02:10.560
The idea is to minimize risk.

00:02:10.560 --> 00:02:12.400
- Supply chain problems
happen all the time.

00:02:12.400 --> 00:02:14.290
The most common pre-pandemic

00:02:14.290 --> 00:02:16.520
was something to do
with a natural disaster

00:02:16.520 --> 00:02:18.600
or a hurricane or a typhoon

00:02:18.600 --> 00:02:21.750
or something affects the ability

00:02:21.750 --> 00:02:25.570
to get stuff on the shelves
of your local grocery stores

00:02:25.570 --> 00:02:26.930
so things are emptied out

00:02:26.930 --> 00:02:28.950
and then that has a ripple effect

00:02:28.950 --> 00:02:31.320
on the cost of transporting goods.

00:02:31.320 --> 00:02:33.660
- [Narrator] So if a
company is regionalized,

00:02:33.660 --> 00:02:35.760
a closed factory in one place

00:02:35.760 --> 00:02:39.470
only impacts sales in nearby locations.

00:02:39.470 --> 00:02:42.140
Companies are also looking at
shortening their supply chains

00:02:42.140 --> 00:02:45.010
through a similar strategy
called nearshoring.

00:02:45.010 --> 00:02:47.010
The concept is pretty simple.

00:02:47.010 --> 00:02:49.240
It refers to the practice
of pulling production

00:02:49.240 --> 00:02:50.950
that had been moved far away,

00:02:50.950 --> 00:02:52.260
back to a country closer

00:02:52.260 --> 00:02:55.260
to where the products
are distributed and sold.

00:02:55.260 --> 00:02:57.523
And this strategy is
becoming more popular.

00:02:58.370 --> 00:02:59.640
After facing shortages,

00:02:59.640 --> 00:03:01.880
the Italian clothing company, Benetton,

00:03:01.880 --> 00:03:03.900
decided to increase manufacturing

00:03:03.900 --> 00:03:07.520
in Serbia, Croatia,
Turkey, Tunisia, and Egypt,

00:03:07.520 --> 00:03:10.280
closer to where the
products are sold in Europe.

00:03:10.280 --> 00:03:11.710
It's a big move.

00:03:11.710 --> 00:03:14.360
The company plans to cut
its Asia production by half

00:03:14.360 --> 00:03:16.200
over the next year or so.

00:03:16.200 --> 00:03:18.320
It's a reversal of the decades long shift

00:03:18.320 --> 00:03:19.750
by many apparel companies

00:03:19.750 --> 00:03:23.480
to Asian factories that
offered low cost supplies.

00:03:23.480 --> 00:03:26.900
Some companies are also using
a strategy called reshoring.

00:03:26.900 --> 00:03:29.410
It's similar to nearshoring,
but in this case,

00:03:29.410 --> 00:03:32.680
a company that previously
moved manufacturing overseas

00:03:32.680 --> 00:03:34.040
moves it back to the country

00:03:34.040 --> 00:03:36.370
where it was originally located.

00:03:36.370 --> 00:03:40.780
- With reshoring, a
business may choose to move

00:03:40.780 --> 00:03:42.720
some of its manufacturing

00:03:42.720 --> 00:03:46.450
that it had been doing
elsewhere back to the US

00:03:46.450 --> 00:03:51.190
if it had experienced trouble
getting the products here,

00:03:51.190 --> 00:03:53.870
whether there wasn't enough
production capacity abroad,

00:03:53.870 --> 00:03:56.030
or whether there were
transportation bottlenecks

00:03:56.030 --> 00:03:57.890
that were preventing products

00:03:57.890 --> 00:04:01.030
from getting here when they were needed.

00:04:01.030 --> 00:04:02.020
- [Narrator] While moving production

00:04:02.020 --> 00:04:04.110
could solve some supply chain issues,

00:04:04.110 --> 00:04:08.110
these solutions aren't a fix-all
and they still carry risks.

00:04:08.110 --> 00:04:11.200
Mainly, they're expensive to pull off.

00:04:11.200 --> 00:04:12.960
According to a 2020 report,

00:04:12.960 --> 00:04:15.880
the total combined cost for
US and European companies

00:04:15.880 --> 00:04:17.980
to move manufacturing out of China

00:04:17.980 --> 00:04:21.803
would come to some $1 trillion
over the next five years.

00:04:22.870 --> 00:04:24.610
And for many smaller companies,

00:04:24.610 --> 00:04:26.650
the costs are too high to bear.

00:04:26.650 --> 00:04:29.770
- Some companies may not have enough money

00:04:29.770 --> 00:04:32.210
to set up manufacturing domestically.

00:04:32.210 --> 00:04:34.880
And even if they did,
it might not make sense

00:04:34.880 --> 00:04:37.670
because their product might
end up costing twice as much

00:04:37.670 --> 00:04:38.503
as it does.

00:04:38.503 --> 00:04:42.550
So they are gonna have to
weigh the cost of delays

00:04:42.550 --> 00:04:44.630
of a product that's made
elsewhere getting here.

00:04:44.630 --> 00:04:46.420
What does that mean for their bottom line

00:04:46.420 --> 00:04:48.970
versus would they have as
many buyers for that product

00:04:48.970 --> 00:04:51.510
if they were making it domestically

00:04:51.510 --> 00:04:53.763
and it costs twice as much?

00:04:54.680 --> 00:04:56.880
- [Narrator] Overhauling
entire supply chains

00:04:56.880 --> 00:04:59.100
can also take years.
(horn honking)

00:04:59.100 --> 00:05:01.560
Still, some companies
think these strategies

00:05:01.560 --> 00:05:03.750
could pay dividends for the future

00:05:03.750 --> 00:05:06.066
and help their businesses survive.

00:05:06.066 --> 00:05:09.233
(bright lively music)

